\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{How the cube models connect}

\p{An abstract cube turn records incidence positions and restricts a shape
symmetry to one facet. A layered-cube turn records cubies, stickers, frames
and allowed layers. For the ordinary three-layer cube these are two
descriptions of the same action; the map below identifies their coordinates.}

\subtree{
  \title{From facet incidence to the 54-sticker state}

  \p{The facet calculation in [one ordinary cube face turn](frap-000P)
  selects the facet-colored part of the incidence set. Put the cube's surface
  cells at
  #{p\in\{-1,0,1\}^3\ \text{with}\ \lVert p\rVert_\infty=1}.
  A visible sticker is a pair #{(p,n)}, where #{n=\operatorname{sgn}(p_i)e_i}
  for each nonzero coordinate #{p_i}. Thus a corner has three visible
  normals, an edge has two, and a face center has one:}
  ##{8\cdot3+12\cdot2+6=54.}
  \p{The vertex--facet incidences in the facet-colored set map to the pairs
  above by sending a cube vertex, edge, or facet to its surface-cell coordinate
  and its incident facet normal. Conversely, the zero-coordinate pattern of
  #{p} determines the cubie (three nonzero entries for a
  \vocabk{corner}{frap-0003}, two for an \vocabk{edge}{frap-0003}, one for a
  centre), and #{n} determines the incident facet, so this is
  a bijection between the 54 incidences and the 54 pairs. The cubie counts and
  this incidence decomposition follow the general model in
  \citet{Sections 2--3}{bonzio2018nxnxn}; the displayed pairing and 54-count
  are the calculation used here. For #{N=3}, group these pairs into eight
  corner and twelve edge \newvocab{sticker fibres}, leaving the six center
  positions as fixed frame marks. A legal outer-face move preserves this
  partition and carries the cyclic labels in a corner fibre, or the ordered labels in an
  edge fiber, to the corresponding destination fiber. Write
  #{C_p=\{(p,\operatorname{sgn}(p_i)e_i):p_i\ne0\}} for a corner fibre and
  #{E_p} for an edge fibre. Within #{C_p}, order the three normals by the
  coordinate axes #{e_1,e_2,e_3} and read them cyclically; within #{E_p}, use
  increasing axis order. Identify those ordered labels with the U/D and F/B
  frame labels of the [complete three-layer cube](frap-0016). This fixes each
  per-fibre bijection, so the destination permutation and local frame changes
  are defined without an unstated convention. Therefore it induces exactly
  the fixed-frame coordinates}
  ##{(\sigma,x;\tau,z)\in S_8\times(\mathbb Z_3)^8
        \times S_{12}\times(\mathbb Z_2)^{12}}
  \p{used in [the complete three-layer cube](frap-0016). More explicitly, if
  a move sends a corner fiber #{C_p} to #{C_{p'}}, set #{\sigma(p)=p'};
  the unique cyclic offset of the three normals gives #{x_p\in\mathbb Z_3}.
  The analogous destination edge fiber and its ordered-normal offset give
  #{\tau} and #{z\in(\mathbb Z_2)^{12}}. For the positive-#{z} turn
  #{q(x,y,z)=(y,-x,z)} from [one ordinary cube face turn](frap-000P),}
  ##{((1,1,1),e_z)\longmapsto((1,-1,1),e_z),\qquad
      ((1,1,1),e_y)\longmapsto((1,-1,1),e_x).}
  \p{This exhibits both the destination corner and the transported local
  frame. The induced map is a change of state description for the legal
  action: it sends a sticker permutation to its corner and edge destination
  permutations together with the local frame changes. It does not assert that
  every permutation of the 54 positions is a legal cube move.}

  \p{This also separates two kinds of forgetting. Passing from the incidence
  action to the displayed coordinates forgets the individual sticker
  addresses inside each recorded local frame. Passing from labelled cubies to
  an ordinary colour observation forgets equal-looking labels. The latter is
  the observation quotient discussed in [which moves survive a quotient](frap-0019),
  and it is not part of the abstract-to-coordinate handoff.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{What changes when layers are added}

  \p{For larger #{N}, the same surface-cell description retains the corner
  and edge fibers but adds coupled-edge and center orbits. Inner layers move
  those new fibers, and even and odd sizes have different orbit signatures.
  The all-#{N} coordinates in [the layered-cube law](frap-0004) therefore
  extend the three-layer state description rather than replace it. Their
  parity and orientation equations are stated for a labelled fixed-frame
  model; they are not obtained by treating every center sticker as one of the
  twelve ordinary edges.}

  \p{The transition is consequently a specialization in one direction:
  the #{N=3} outer-layer case of [the family of layered cubes](frap-0003)
  gives the ordinary fixed-frame action, while the abstract facet action
  explains why that outer turn moves precisely the selected surface fibers.
  Whole-cube rotations, marked centers, central slices and colour-only
  identifications are additional choices, not silent changes of coordinates.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{What the identification supports}

  \p{The explicit identification supports this chain of state descriptions:}
  \ol{
    \li{incidence, flags and a local facet action explain what a move is;}
    \li{the layered model and its first law explain which fibres and
      constraints appear as #{N} changes;}
    \li{the fixed-frame three-layer coordinates make the three familiar
      constraints and their constructive sufficiency explicit;}
    \li{the human and computer chapters use the same state action while
      assigning different meanings to preservation, cost and optimality;}
    \li{the return-to-play account records how a declared move word is
      replayed and checked against those models.}
  }

  \p{Three linked comparisons use the same distinctions. Label transport and
  branched unfolding study return maps on simplex labels; compatible quotients
  test whether an identification of sticker observations inherits a move; and
  scrambling and spectra study which functions retain memory under a declared
  move law. These are comparisons of transport, forgetting and randomization,
  not identifications with the fixed-frame cube action.}
}
